      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season the lamb chunks with the black pepper and 1 tsp of ras el hanout. Heat the olive oil in a dutch oven over high heat and brown the lamb in batches for 3-4 minutes per batch. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to medium and add the chopped onions to the pot. Cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring, until softened and starting to colour. Add the garlic and the remaining 1 tsp of ras el hanout and cook for 1 further minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the lamb to the pot along with the cinnamon stick. Pour in 400ml of water, bring to a simmer, then cover and cook over the lowest heat for 1 hour."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the dates and honey to the pot. Stir gently, replace the lid and cook for a further 20-25 minutes until the lamb is tender and the dates have softened into the sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Meanwhile, toast the flaked almonds in a dry frying pan over medium heat for 2-3 minutes until golden. Watch them closely as they colour quickly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove the cinnamon stick. Taste and adjust the seasoning. Serve topped with the toasted almonds and fresh coriander alongside couscous or flatbread."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The lamb is still tough after 90 minutes"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lamb shoulder needs a very low, steady heat to break down properly. If it is still tough, simply continue cooking with the lid on for another 20-30 minutes. The pieces will soften with more time."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The dates have dissolved completely"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "This is not a problem. Dissolved dates thicken the sauce and add sweetness. If you want visible fruit in the finished dish, add the dates in the last 15 minutes only."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "question": "The sauce is too sweet"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Balance the sweetness with a squeeze of fresh lemon juice at the end of cooking. Start with half a lemon and taste as you go. A small pinch of extra black pepper also helps counter sweetness."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sweet meat tagines are a hallmark of Moroccan celebratory cooking, with the sweet-savoury combination reflecting the influence of Andalusian cooking brought to Morocco centuries ago. "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "ras-el-hanout"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "Ras el hanout"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is the defining spice blend here, its warmth threading through the braising liquid and into the meat. Versions using prunes or figs appear across the country, with almonds almost always added for crunch. This is feast food, made for weddings and family gatherings, though it works just as well on an ordinary weeknight."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
